<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
<Document id="root_doc">
<name>coast1899 — Newborough Warren, 1899 high-water mark and dune edge</name>
<description><![CDATA[
Two lines digitised from a georeferenced historic Ordnance Survey sheet:
  id=1  High_tide  — High Water Mark of Ordinary Tides (seaward line)
  id=2  Dune_edge  — landward dune edge; mean separation 124 m
The two are also flagged in each placemark's High_tide / Dune_edge SimpleData.
id=2 is the line comparable with the modern seaward-edge series
(coast2006.kml, coast2020.kml) — dune edge against dune edge.

SOURCE MAP
  Ordnance Survey, Anglesey Sheet XXV.NW, revised 1899, published 1901.
  Reproduced with the permission of the National Library of Scotland
  (https://maps.nls.uk). Licensed CC-BY-NC-SA 4.0.
  This attribution is owed by this file and by anything derived from it.

DIGITISING AND GEOREFERENCING
  Raster georeferenced in QGIS, February 2022, to EPSG:27700 (OSGB36 /
  British National Grid): 27 ground control points, RMSE 5.95 m
  (3.27 m excluding two outliers of 23.2 m and 12.2 m).
  Lines digitised by Martin Hollingham, August 2026.
  Renamed coast1900 -> coast1899 on 2026-08-28: the file is named for the
  survey revision date, which is the epoch a retreat rate needs, not for the
  1901 publication date.
  Coordinates in this file are WGS84, as the KML format requires.
  The source raster is NOT in this repository, by decision D-081; it is held
  in data/geo/histmaps/ locally and archived outside the repository.

See data/geo/GEO_PROVENANCE.md for the full record.
]]></description>
